# ============================================================================
# Phase 12 — environment template
#
# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in. NEVER commit `.env.local` (gitignored).
#
# Every integration below reports REAL / SANDBOX / NOT CONFIGURED at runtime on
# /api/health. An unset variable disables that integration — it never falls
# back to a mock or a fake success (spec §24).
# ============================================================================

# --- Core -------------------------------------------------------------------
NODE_ENV=development
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# PostgreSQL 14+ connection string. Vercel Postgres / Neon / Supabase all work.
#   postgres://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/DB?sslmode=require&connection_limit=5
#
# --- CONNECTION POOLING (read this before you deploy) -----------------------
#
# A Vercel Function is a short-lived, horizontally-scaled process. Under card
# traffic the failure mode is NOT "the database is slow" — it is "the database
# ran out of connection slots". The Postgres server has a hard ceiling
# (max_connections, typically 20-100 on a small plan) and EVERY warm function
# instance holds one or more connections open for its whole lifetime. Ten
# concurrent checkout requests across ten instances is ten connections; a minute
# of a traffic spike can consume the entire pool and starve every other request
# with `P1001: Can't reach database server` / `too many clients already`.
#
# So: point DATABASE_URL at a POOLED endpoint and add explicit driver limits.
#
# 1. Neon  -> use the "-pooled" hostname from the Neon console. It fronts
#             PgBouncer for you. (A plain Neon URL is safe on the free tier
#             because it uses a serverless driver, but the pooled URL is the
#             portable choice.)
# 2. Supabase -> use the "Connection pooler" host shown on the dashboard, NOT
#             the direct `db.<ref>.supabase.co` host. It is also PgBouncer, in
#             transaction mode. Session-level features (`SET` outside a
#             transaction, LISTEN/NOTIFY, temp tables, advisory locks that live
#             past a statement) do not work through it — Prisma's interactive
#             transactions and `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` DO, because
#             both execute entirely inside one transaction.
# 3. Vercel Postgres -> the connection strings it hands you ALREADY pool.
#             Leave them alone. Adding `connection_limit` is still fine, but
#             rewriting the URL or inserting pgbouncer yourself is not.
# 4. Self-managed Postgres -> put PgBouncer (or Supavisor) in front of it and
#             use the PgBouncer port (6432), not 5432.
#
# Query parameters Prisma's `postgresql` connector understands:
#
#   ?sslmode=require
#       MANDATORY IN PRODUCTION. Remote Postgres refuses plaintext auth on a
#       non-TLS connection; without this you get `no pg_hba.conf entry` on the
#       first query from the deployed environment even though the same URL works
#       locally. On Supabase use `sslmode=require` (or `verify-full` if you want
#       the CA pinned — `require` encrypts but does not verify the certificate).
#
#   &connection_limit=N
#       Max sockets ONE function instance may open. This is the single most
#       important knob on serverless. Keep it SMALL (1-5). The instance count is
#       Vercel's to scale; your ceiling is
#         max_connections / connection_limit  ==  safe max concurrent functions.
#       connection_limit=20 with 50 warm instances = 1000 sockets = guaranteed
#       exhaustion. connection_limit=1 with the same 50 instances = 50.
#       Choose the number as: (database max_connections - headroom for your own
#       tooling and migrations) / expected concurrent instances.
#
#   &pool_timeout=N
#       How long the driver waits for a free connection before giving up,
#       in seconds. Too LOW turns normal pooling queueing into 5xx errors; too
#       HIGH makes requests pile up until Vercel's own maxDuration kills them
#       (a customer sees a failed payment, not a retryable error). 10-30 is the
#       sane band; the default (10) is fine for a $3 SKU.
#
#   &connect_timeout=N
#       TCP/TLS connect timeout. Keep it small (5-10) so an unreachable database
#       fails fast into our retry path instead of holding a function instance.
#
#   &pgbouncer=true
#       Required ONLY for the `postgres` (node-postgres) driver behind PgBouncer.
#       Prisma's own `pg` adapter infers this from the :6432 port; set it
#       explicitly if you are pinning a custom port.
#
# WORKED EXAMPLES
#   Supabase pooled (recommended):
#     postgres://postgres.PROJECT:PASSWORD@aws-0-us-east-1.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?sslmode=require&connection_limit=5&pool_timeout=15&connect_timeout=10
#   Neon pooled:
#     postgres://user:PASSWORD@ep-xxx-pooler.aws-us-east-1.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require&connection_limit=5&pool_timeout=15&connect_timeout=10
#   Self-managed + PgBouncer:
#     postgres://app_user:PASSWORD@db.internal:6432/redeem?sslmode=require&connection_limit=5&pool_timeout=15
#
# NOTHING in this app opens an unbounded pool, so if you are unsure: start at
# connection_limit=5 and pool_timeout=15 and tune from observed errors, not
# from theory. See DEPLOY.md §9.
DATABASE_URL=""

# --- Encryption (REQUIRED) --------------------------------------------------
# AES-256-GCM key for redeem-code inventory at rest. 32 bytes, base64 or hex.
# Generate: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
#   OR: openssl rand -base64 32
# ROTATING THIS INVALIDATES ALL EXISTING INVENTORY — there is no recovery.
ENCRYPTION_KEY=""

# HMAC key for deterministic fingerprints (duplicate-code detection, PII
# hashing). Falls back to ENCRYPTION_KEY if unset. Use a DIFFERENT random value.
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
FINGERPRINT_KEY=""

# Secret for signing admin/user session cookies.
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
SESSION_SECRET=""

# First admin account, created by `pnpm db:seed`. Change before first login.
ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_EMAIL="owner@example.com"
ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD=""

# --- Payment provider: Whop -------------------------------------------------
# 1. Go to https://sandbox.whop.com/dashboard/developer (sandbox)
#    or https://whop.com/dashboard/developer (production)
# 2. Create an API key. Required scopes:
#      checkout_configuration:create      -> create checkout links
#      checkout_configuration:basic:read   -> read checkout metadata
#      payment:basic:read                 -> verify payments, reconcile
#      member:email:read                  -> read buyer email (reconciliation)
#      webhook_receive:payments           -> receive webhooks
# 3. Your Whop account id (biz_...) from Settings.
WHOP_API_KEY=""
WHOP_ACCOUNT_ID=""

# Set to `sandbox` while testing. Controls the base URL and the reported
# status. Production uses https://api.whop.com/api/v1.
WHOP_ENVIRONMENT=sandbox

# Pinning this is not optional: unpinned requests silently return the
# 2025-01-01 response shape, where the account field is `company_id`.
WHOP_API_VERSION_DATE=2026-10-07-2

# Shown ONCE when you create the webhook in the Whop dashboard. Starts with `ws_`.
# Register the webhook URL as:  {NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL}/api/payments/webhook
# and subscribe to:
#   payment.succeeded, payment.failed, payment.pending, payment.canceled
#   refund.created, refund.updated
#   dispute.created, dispute.updated, dispute_alert.created
WHOP_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""

# Reject webhook timestamps older than this. Whop docs specify 300 seconds.
WEBHOOK_TOLERANCE_SECONDS=300

# --- Payment methods (spec §7) ---------------------------------------------
# Whop's hosted checkout settles CARD only. UPI/WALLET/NETBANKING are declared
# unsupported by the provider rather than faked.
ENABLED_PAYMENT_METHODS=CARD

# --- Email (spec §12) -------------------------------------------------------
# EMAIL_PROVIDER = SES | RESEND | SMTP
#
# SES is the cheapest at scale (~$0.10/1000) and has the best transactional
# deliverability, which matters because a code in spam = an unredeemable paid
# order. It requires a verified domain and the address is out of sandbox.
# RESEND has a 3000/month free tier and is the fastest to set up.
EMAIL_PROVIDER=SES
EMAIL_FROM="orders@yourdomain.com"
EMAIL_FROM_NAME="Your Store"
EMAIL_REPLY_TO="support@yourdomain.com"

# --- Option A: Amazon SES ---
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=""
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=""
AWS_REGION=us-east-1

# --- Option B: Resend (alternative to SES) ---
# Set EMAIL_PROVIDER=RESEND and fill this instead of the AWS_* block.
# RESEND_API_KEY="re_xxxxxxxx"

# --- Option C: generic SMTP (alternative) ---
# SMTP_HOST=""
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USER=""
# SMTP_PASSWORD=""
# SMTP_SECURE=false

# --- Redis / rate limiting (spec §16, §17) ----------------------------------
# Upstash REST is serverless-friendly. Without it, rate limiting degrades to
# in-memory per-instance (documented limitation, not a silent no-op).
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=""
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=""

# --- Durable job queue: Inngest (spec §16, §21) -----------------------------
# Inngest is required for the fulfillment pipeline: Vercel has no long-lived
# workers, so steps must survive a cold start. Without it, fulfillment does not
# run and /api/health reports NOT CONFIGURED.
#
# Create a free app at https://app.inngest.com, then:
INNGEST_EVENT_KEY=""
INNGEST_SIGNING_KEY=""

# --- Observability (spec §22) ----------------------------------------------
# debug | info | warn | error. Defaults to `info` in production.
LOG_LEVEL=info

# Bearer token guarding GET /api/metrics (Prometheus scrape endpoint).
#
# UNSET MEANS THE ENDPOINT DOES NOT EXIST: the route returns 404, not 401, so a
# prober cannot learn that metrics were ever built. There is no "open metrics"
# mode and there is deliberately no query-string fallback (a token in a URL
# ends up in access logs, proxy logs and browser history).
# Generate a 32+ byte random value:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
# Scrape with:  curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $METRICS_TOKEN" https://yourdomain.com/api/metrics
METRICS_TOKEN=""

# --- Vercel cron authentication (spec §23) ---------------------------------
# Vercel Cron sends `Authorization: Bearer ${CRON_SECRET}` on every scheduled
# request, where ${CRON_SECRET} is the variable value in the Vercel project.
#
# UNSET MEANS THE CRON ROUTES DO NOT EXIST: /api/cron/* answers 404 rather than
# 401. That is deliberate. An unauthenticated cron endpoint is a public button
# that triggers reconciliation and fulfillment sweeps, so it must be absent, not
# merely closed. It also stops Vercel's cron-retry noise.
#
# UNIQUE PER ENVIRONMENT. Do not reuse CRON_SECRET between Preview and
# Production: Preview deployments get their own cron trigger, and a shared
# secret would make every preview a valid trigger for production data.
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
#
# This gates three routes, scheduled in vercel.json:
#   0 * * * *      /api/cron/inventory-reaper      releases expired reservations
#   */5 * * * *   /api/cron/fulfillment-recovery  re-runs stalled fulfillments
#   17 3 * * *    /api/cron/reconcile              daily provider reconciliation
#
# Test a cron locally without waiting for the schedule:
#   curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET" https://yourdomain.com/api/cron/reconcile
CRON_SECRET=""

# --- Reconciliation (spec §18) ---------------------------------------------
# Window used on the very first reconciliation run, in hours.
RECONCILIATION_LOOKBACK_HOURS=72

# --- Business rules (spec §3) ----------------------------------------------
# Defaults applied when no PricingRule row exists. Basis points (500 = 5%).
DEFAULT_MIN_DISCOUNT_BPS=500
DEFAULT_MIN_MARGIN_BPS=0

# --- Optional tuning --------------------------------------------------------
# Every variable below is OPTIONAL. Unset, the system uses the documented
# default and /api/health stays honest. None of them are required to boot.

# Displayed as the issuer name in authenticator apps during admin MFA enrolment.
# MFA_ISSUER="Your Store"

# Catalog page cache lifetime in seconds. Read by src/catalog/cache-control.ts.
# CATALOG_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS=60

# Currency used for the Whop dispute/chargeback fee in margin maths.
# Whop charges ~$15 per dispute; getting this wrong skews reported margin.
# WHOP_DISPUTE_FEE_CURRENCY=USD

# Currency the alert evaluator thresholds are expressed in (lowercase ok).
# ALERT_CURRENCY=usd

# Set to "false" to stop persisting Alert rows to Postgres. Only useful when
# the alert table is the bottleneck; alerts still fire, they are just not kept.
# ALERT_PERSIST=true

# Override the Inngest dev-server endpoint. LEAVE UNSET ON VERCEL — in production
# Inngest calls back into your own /api/inngest over the public URL. Setting it
# on Vercel points the queue at localhost and fulfillment silently stops.
# INNGEST_BASE_URL=

# Written onto the supplier record by `pnpm db:seed`. Your contract/reference id
# with the authorised supplier. Set it to your real reference before going live.
# SUPPLIER_AUTHORIZATION_REF=

# LOCAL DEVELOPMENT ONLY — DO NOT SET THIS ON VERCEL.
# When present, the test helpers REPLACE DATABASE_URL with it and the
# DB-backed concurrency and end-to-end suites un-skip. Pointing this at your
# production database means `pnpm test` truncates and rewrites real orders.
#   TEST_DATABASE_URL="postgres://.../redeem_test?sslmode=require"
TEST_DATABASE_URL=